# Description rewrite brief — rebuilding a row's `Description` from its shrine entry

**Ruled by Rauf, 13 October 2026, in chat (HANDOVER §9.333).** He chose *rewrite from the entries*
over *extend the current text*, starting with the rows that have row-specific material from two
or more books (94 on 13 October; the list is `out/descriptions/rows_2plus.json`, regenerated by
`pipeline/book_queue/description_queue.py`). His standing guidance on the result:

- **"The more information we are able to capture the better."** Data Darbar's current Description
  (~15,000 characters) "is a good length but ideally even longer would be better". Write everything
  the row's entry supports. Length comes from material, never from padding: a row whose books say
  little gets a shorter article, and that is correct.
- **More poetry.** Quote the verse the entry carries: couplets, hymns, chronograms, the poet-saint's
  own lines (rules under "Verse" below).
- **Enrich existing rows first, then new rows.** These rewrites are that first step.

- **Long articles are split, never cut** (Rauf, 13 October 2026, §9.334). A Google Sheets cell holds at
  most 50,000 characters, and the Bulleh Shah pilot ran to 72,000. There is no cap on the article;
  instead **every `## ` section must stay under 45,000 characters** (checker F6), and at import the
  article is divided by section between `Description` and the site's dedicated article columns
  (`ARTICLE_SECTION_DEFINITIONS` in `src/lib/data/constants.ts`). Write one whole article; the
  splitter is a separate, scripted step.
- **The pilot was approved** (13 October 2026): `shrine-of-shah-yusaf-gardez` (9.7k, a thin row with
  an unassigned notice given as a hedged pointer), `tilla-jogian` (31k), `mazar-of-bulleh-shah`
  (72k, sixteen disagreements). Read one of them before drafting your first row.

This brief is for whoever drafts, a scheduled firing or a worker it briefs. Read it whole. CLAUDE.md
RULE 2 governs every sentence.

## What you read

1. `shrine_entries/<id>.md`, the whole file. It holds:
   - `## Current site description (as of …)` (only on entries the integration created), or the
     older title-named entry's own prose sections, followed by
   - one `## From <Author, Short title>` section per book, copied verbatim from that book's
     takeaways, with `### Doubts the notes raise on this row`, and the `### Province-level`,
     `### Place-level` and `### Person-level material` blocks.
2. The row's live cells: `data/snapshot_2026-10-08_post-import.csv` until a fresher export exists,
   for `Name`, `Description` (the CURRENT text, the one you are rewriting), `category`,
   `principal_figure`, `figure_died` and the rest. Read the current `Description` from here, not
   from the entry: older entries do not carry it.
3. For each book you cite, the `*Source: …*` line at the top of
   `entries/book_takeaways/<slug>.md` (author, title, language, date, edition). The slug is in the
   section's `*Integrated … from \`entries/book_takeaways/<slug>.md\`*` line.

## What goes in

- **Everything in the current Description.** It is what readers see now and is cited by its own
  bibliography. Keep every claim it makes, rewritten into the new article. Drop a claim only where
  a book contradicts it, and even then **do not drop it: state both, side by side** ("The site's
  account gives his death as 1757; Rafat's selection gives 1758 (…)"). Never reconcile.
- **Every row-specific item in the book sections**: the bullets under Saint, Shrine / Darbar,
  Practices & Events, Legends & Miracles, Significance, Other, and the Doubts. Each fact carries
  its citation (below).
- **Place-, person- and province-level material only as context, framed as context.** It speaks
  of the city, the province or the figure, not this site: "Kanhaiya Lal's history of Lahore
  counts …", never "the shrine has …". Use it for a short `## Setting` passage if it adds
  something; never let it attribute a fact to this site.
- **Legends as legends**: "Lahori relates that …", "tradition, as Sharib records it, holds …".
  Never in the narrator's own voice.

## What stays out

- **Anything not in the entry or the current Description.** Not a date, a silsila, a building
  detail or a "well-known" fact from general knowledge. If the material does not say it, the
  article does not say it. If an obvious question is left open (no urs date, no builder), you may
  say the sources consulted do not record it.
- **Material the entry itself does not assign to this row.** Where a bullet or a Doubt says *not
  assigned*, *tentative*, *must stay unmapped*, or the only link is a hedge ("a possible
  reference", "associated"), do not present it as this row's. Either leave it out, or, if the
  pointer is worth a reader's knowing, give it as a pointer with its uncertainty: "Hadiqat
  al-Awliya has an entry on a 'Shah Gardez of Multan' that may be this saint; it gives him no
  personal name (…)". Record which you did in the notes file.
- **Claim-noted material** (`*Attached as this book's claim …*`, §9.317): it may go in, always as
  that book's claim, with the doubt beside it.
- **Process words.** Never write "the notes", "the takeaways", "chunk", "Doubt I23", "the archive
  row", "integrated", "OCR", "PDF page", "worker". A reader sees an article. Uncertainty is
  expressed in plain words: "the reading of the year is uncertain in the printed text".
- **Description text for any row outside the 94** until Rauf widens the scope, and any row
  outside Pakistan (§9.284).

## Citations

Inline, in parentheses, at the end of the sentence or clause they support:
`(Lahori, *Hadiqat al-Awliya*, p. 276, folio 264)` — the author's surname as the `## From` heading
gives it, the title in italics as the heading gives it, then the page reference **exactly as the
entry gives it** (`p. N`, `p. N, folio M`, `p. N, folio not stated`). Never invent or tidy a page
number, never turn an index or endnote locator into a page, and carry `[OCR?]` on a digit if the
entry has it. Two books for one fact: `(Kugle, *Sufis and Saints' Bodies*, p. 12; Rafat, *Bulleh
Shah: A Selection*, p. 4)`. A sentence from the current Description keeps no inline citation
unless it already had one; its sources stay in the bibliography.

**The cited title must be a substring of the `## From` heading** (the checker finds the book's
section that way), and **the same string must appear in that book's bibliography item** (B2). Where
the heading and the Source line differ (`Bulleh Shah: Sufi Lyrics` against *Sufi Lyrics*), cite the
shorter form both contain. Short quotations (under six words) may take single quotes; double quotes
are paired in order by the checker, so never leave one unmatched.

## Quotation

- **Urdu, Persian, Punjabi, Sindhi and Arabic quotations verbatim, as printed in the entry**:
  spelling as printed (the dotted final nūn and all), `[OCR?]` and `[illegible]` carried. Remove
  only the entry's own `**bold**` emphasis markers inside a quotation.
- **English quotations verbatim** from the entry's quotation of the book.
- Give an English sense of a non-English quotation where the entry glosses it (the bullets usually
  do). Where it does not, say what the passage is about in the entry's words. Never produce your
  own translation of verse.

## Verse ("more poetry")

Rauf wants more of the poetry the books carry. For each row, look for: the saint's own verse,
verse about the saint or the shrine, chronograms (*tarikh*) with the year they encode, hymns and
*kafis* sung there, inscriptions in verse.

- **Original-script verse** goes in as a verse paragraph: hemistichs or lines separated by single
  newlines, every line in Arabic script, and nothing else in the paragraph (no citation, no Latin,
  no list marker). `src/components/shrine/ProseParagraphs.tsx` renders exactly that shape as a
  centred verse block and anything else as prose, so the shape is not optional.
- **The English** follows as its own paragraph: in italics, then the citation. Join lines with
  ` / ` **only where the entry gives the translation's line divisions**; where it prints the
  translation as running text, quote it as running text (the pilot put slashes at sentence ends,
  which invents a lineation the translator did not print). Use the book's own translation where the book is a translation (Shackle, Rafat,
  Usborne, Alam, Sorley, Schimmel and the like); otherwise the entry's gloss, or a plain statement
  of the verse's subject.
- **Gurmukhi or Devanagari verse** (Janamsakhi and hymn material) is not rendered as a verse
  block; quote it inline in one paragraph with lines joined by ` / `.
- **Keep excerpts short**: a couplet or a stanza, a handful of lines, never a whole poem. Modern
  translations are their translators' work, and an excerpt cited is what a scholarly article
  uses. Several short excerpts across an article are better than one long one.
- A chronogram keeps the year the book says it encodes, its digits as printed.

## Shape

Markdown, matching the archive's existing articles (headings by frequency in the live sheet:
Bibliography, Overview, Legacy, The Life of the Saint, The Shrine…). Use the headings the material
supports, in roughly this order, and rename them to fit the tradition (The Gurdwara, The Temple,
The Goddess, The Guru's Visit):

`## Overview` · `## The Life of the Saint` · `## The Shrine` (founding, fabric, custodians,
endowments) · `## Devotion and Practice` (urs, rituals, music, langar, pilgrimage) ·
`## Legends and Miracles` · `## In Verse` (if the verse is not better placed in the narrative) ·
`## Setting` (place-level context, optional) · `## Where the Sources Disagree` · `## Legacy` ·
`## Bibliography`

`## Where the Sources Disagree` is required whenever two sources, or the current text and a source,
differ on anything: a date, a name, an identification, a miracle's details. One bullet or short
paragraph per disagreement, every side cited, no verdict.

**Formatting invariants** (checked by `pipeline/book_queue/check_description_draft.py`):

- One paragraph per line, a blank line between paragraphs. Single newlines only inside a verse
  paragraph or between `- ` list items.
- `## ` headings only (no `#`, no `###`), each on its own paragraph.
- Asterisks balanced in every paragraph; `*italics*` for book titles and terms (*ʿurs*, *langar*).
- No HTML, no footnote syntax, no tables, no links other than URLs already in the bibliography.
- Honorifics as in `data/glossary.csv` (Hazrat, Shah, Baba, Guru, Sant …), names spelled as the
  current Description spells them, with the book's spelling in a citation-bearing clause if it
  differs.

**Bibliography**: every item the current Description lists, verbatim and first, then one `- `
item per book cited, built from that book's `*Source: …*` line: `- Author, *Title* (language,
date; edition/publisher as the Source line gives it).` Add nothing the Source line does not give.

## What you write, and where

For each row `<id>`:

1. `entries/descriptions/<id>.md` — the proposed Description, exactly the cell's text and nothing
   else (no front matter, no heading above `## Overview`).
2. `entries/descriptions/<id>.notes.md` — for Rauf's review: what was left out and why (with the
   entry line numbers), every pointer you gave in hedged form, every disagreement you listed,
   every `[OCR?]` you carried into the prose, the verse you quoted and from where, and anything
   that needs his decision (RULE 5: in a scheduled run, list it; interactively, ask).

Then run `python3 pipeline/book_queue/check_description_draft.py <id>`. It must exit 0. It checks
the formatting invariants; that every citation's page reference occurs in that book's section of
the entry; that every Arabic-script run of three or more words occurs verbatim in the entry or the
current Description; that every English quotation of six or more words does too; that the
bibliography keeps every current item; and that nothing from the process vocabulary leaks. **If
it fails, fix the draft, not the check** (RULE 4), unless the check is wrong, in which case fix
the check and say so in the run record.

## After drafting

`python3 pipeline/book_queue/build_description_patch.py` collects every draft that passes into
`data/review/PATCH_descriptions_<stamp>.csv` (`id,Name,current_chars,proposed_chars,books_cited,
Description`) plus a `.md` summary. Rauf reviews before anything is imported; a row he approves
is listed in `data/review/descriptions_approved.txt`, and only listed rows go into the
consolidated import (INV-3 is narrowed to exactly that). Every rewritten English Description
leaves its Urdu article stale (`urdu-i18n/`, `pipeline/urdu_content_qa.py`): the Urdu rewrite is
a later, separate step, never a side effect of this one.
